The paper’s headline result is immediately relevant: Theorem 3 makes tensor-rank decision polynomial-time equivalent to solving polynomial systems, while Observation 8 supplies the NP-hard regime. That supports a Track A family only if the generated distribution preserves that hard structure; a generic “sum random rank-one tensors” plant would certify only an upper bound and would neither prove minimal rank nor inherit distributional hardness. I’m now reading the reductions and proof objects in Sections 4–6 to see whether the paper offers a certificate-preserving construction that avoids that trap.
