codex
Implemented and offline-verified a native tensor-decomposition generator based on [Shitov’s paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.01559):

- [Generator module](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1611.01559/gen_1611_01559.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1611.01559/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1611.01559/README.md)
- [Bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1611.01559/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1611.01559/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1611.01559/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

The family declares Track B. Generation plants XOR shifts and constructs an exact integer rank-one decomposition. Verification recomposes the tensor without reading `inst["answer"]`.

Results:

- G1–G8 pass.
- Four adversarial attacks: 0/8 successes each.
- Reference Walsh–Hadamard algorithm: 966 exact operations, 8/8 success.
- Guessing: 0/200,000; exact probability \(2^{-30}\).
- Answer: 67 characters, 30 atoms.
- Intended route: 192 exact operations.
- Canonical-key audit: 180/180 invariant transformations and carried witnesses passed.

The result is not submission-ready: every bare, hinted, and placebo oracle call returned OpenRouter HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded.” These are correctly recorded as API errors, not oracle failures. Consequently, G9 and `all_passed` remain false pending a usable OpenRouter quota.
